# cppcheck checks turned off for the whole tree (CODING-STANDARD §12, CS-13.3), read by
# tools/analysis/cppcheck.py. Each line is a check id, after a comment that gives its reason.
# A finding at one site is fixed or recorded there (`cppcheck-suppress <id>` with a MISRA Dev
# CS-12 comment), never here: the tool refuses an entry naming a file.

# Advisory MISRA 8.13 (pointer to const where possible). cppcheck judges each configuration
# alone, so it asks for const where another configuration writes through the pointer
# (sched.c's insert() on the tiny profile, AVR and Armv6-M). It cannot see writes through an
# intrusive node (EMB_CONTAINER_OF): a list pop or a wake writes the list through the nodes it
# reaches. Port entry points have contract-fixed signatures (emb_arch_context_init), and public
# functions take the object the caller passes (emb_work_submit). 25 findings at the first run.
constParameterPointer
constVariablePointer

# MISRA C:2012 rules 15.1 and 15.5 (the addon's rule numbers): CODING-STANDARD §12 deviates from
# them everywhere in the product code. A function validates and returns early, and after it
# takes a lock it has one exit through a forward goto to its unlock (CS-7.2).
misra-c2012-15.1
misra-c2012-15.5

# MISRA C:2012 rule 17.3 (no implicit function declaration) is enforced by the compilers:
# GCC 14 and Clang reject an implicit declaration in C11, and CS-13.1's -Wall -Werror fail the
# build on it. The addon reads every inline assembly statement (__asm__ __volatile__, and
# EMB_LAYOUT_MARKER, which expands to one) as a call to an undeclared function: all 43 of its
# findings at the first run were assembly, in the ports and the layout probe (CS-1.5).
misra-c2012-17.3
